\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Off the matching mass}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Take the \vocabk{slab operator}{fdip-01AE} with trivial
transverse links and normal links constant across each layer.
On a nonzero transverse corner space with #{k} components
#{\pi}, put #{d=2k+1-aM}. Write #{m_L} for the least value
of #{\|H_L(U)\psi\|} on its unit sphere. The corner restriction
is invertible exactly when #{d\ne0}, and
##{\frac{|d|^L}{a\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}|d|^j}
 \le m_L \le
\frac{|d|^L}{a\bigl(\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}|d|^{2j}\bigr)^{1/2}}.}}

\proof{
\p{In the layer gauge of \ref{fdip-01AH}, #{aK_{L,U}=d\bbI -N}.
The operator #{N} is a contraction since its two truncated shifts
on #{\im P_\pm} are contractions with orthogonal images.
For #{d\ne0}, #{N^L=0} gives the two-sided inverse
#{\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}d^{-j-1}N^j} by finite multiplication.
The triangle inequality bounds its norm and gives the lower
bound, since #{\Gamma} is unitary. This invertibility also
follows from Mathlib's \leanref{IsNilpotent.isUnit_one_sub}
applied to #{d^{-1}N}.}

\p{Choose a unit value #{v\in\im P_-} in the corner space
on one layer. Put #{\psi_l=d^{l-1}v}. Its norm squared is
#{\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}|d|^{2j}}. All layers of
#{(d\bbI -N)\psi} vanish except the top, with value #{d^Lv}.
This proves the upper bound, including #{d=0}. For
#{0<|d|\le1} the two bounds give
#{|d|^L/(aL)\le m_L\le |d|^L/a}. Thus a uniform positive
lower coefficient on #{|d|^{L-1}} fails for arbitrarily small
nonzero #{d}.}

\p{A normal link that changes character can also produce a
kernel away from every odd mass. Take two layers, one transverse
axis of period #{12}, a scalar fiber and trivial transverse links.
Put #{p=\pi /6}, #{U_s(x,1)^{-1}=\ue^{2\ui px}} and
#{aM=(3-\sqrt{3})/2}. For #{0\ne v\in\im P_-} let
#{w=v-\ui E_t v}, with #{t} the transverse axis. Give the
bottom and top the fields #{\ue^{\ui px}w} and
#{\ue^{-\ui px}w}, respectively. Here #{d(\pm p)=1/2}.
Their on-site factors before grading and division by #{a}
take #{w} to #{(\bbI+\ui E_t)w/2=v} and
#{(\bbI -\ui E_t)w/2=-\ui E_t v}. The two normal hops cancel
these values, since #{P_-w=v}, #{P_+w=-\ui E_t v} and the
normal transport supplies the corresponding characters.}
}
